                     <origDate>Lyon, AD 1532</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#b i"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_109">Cambuskenneth,
                        Stirlingshire. Augustinian abbey of St Mary the Virgin</orgName><p>Ink
                        inscription at the head and foot of the title page 'Biblia M Alexandri
                        Lyon'.</p><p> Gold stamped on to front board: 'R.P. Allex. Abb.
                        Cambuskennet. MDXXXIII.' Gold stamped on to back board: 'Vice Cancellarii.
                        R[e]g. Scoto. MDXXXIII.' Alexander Mylne (c. 1470--1548) was appointed abbot
                        of Cambuskenneth in 1516 </p><p>One of a collection of books looted on 11
                        May 1544 from the Edinburgh townhouse of Cambuskenneth abbey by Sir William
                        Norris of Speke, whose inscription is on the title page. </p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Alexander Mylne (c. 1470--1548), abbot of
                     Cambuskenneth.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sir William Norris of Speke (1501--1568),
                     taken as booty in 1544.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">'William Keyth 2 Septem 1605.',
                     scored-through ink inscription on title page, i.e. William Keith, 6th Earl
                     Marischal (c.1585--1635).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">'Ex Libris Pat. Gordon', 18th-cent. ink
                     inscription.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Found about 1853 in an old cottage at
                     Childwall, near Liverpool, on property belonging to the Marquess of Salisbury,
                     who gifted it to the Liverpool Athenaeum. </provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Purchased from Liverpool Athenaeum by the
                     National Library of Scotland in 2008.</provenance>
               </history>
